## A unique spring region

Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) lies in one of **Europe's richest spring regions**. Some forty mineral springs rise within the town itself, and around a hundred in the surrounding forests and meadows — each with its own character, taste and story. This page is a practical guide: where to find each spring, how to get there, how the water tastes and what to bring with you.

Which spring to drink, in what quantity and when is determined during a drinking cure by the **spa physician** after the initial examination. How the individual waters differ and which complaints they are traditionally prescribed for in Marienbad is explained on the [mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs) page. Here we concentrate on the walk between the springs.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/springs/bronze-fountain-spa-pool.jpg"
   alt="Ornate bronze fountain head pouring water into a spa pool"
   caption="Mineral water flows into the pool through an ornate bronze fountain — a small piece of spa heritage."
   width="wide" /%}

The springs originate in the bedrock of the **Slavkov Forest**. Rainwater seeps deep underground, is enriched with minerals from the surrounding rock and rises back to the surface along tectonic faults together with carbon dioxide of volcanic origin. The water travels through the ground for a long time before it emerges — and that is exactly why it differs so much from ordinary water.

All the Marienbad springs are **cold acidulous waters**: the water rises at a temperature of around **7–10 °C** and contains natural carbon dioxide (CO₂), which gives it its characteristic sparkle. In mineral content, however, the springs differ markedly — some are rich in iron, others in Glauber's salt, others again in calcium or magnesium. That is why each tastes different.

{% treatment-box title="Drinking cure" description="The drinking cure is a traditional part of a spa stay in Marienbad. Guests drink a prescribed quantity of mineral water directly at the spring, usually in the morning before breakfast, slowly and in small sips from a spa cup. The specific spring, quantity and timing are set by the spa physician after the initial examination." icon="water" /%}

## Springs at the colonnade — the heart of the spa quarter

The springs at the [Main Colonnade](/en/colonnade) form the historic centre of spa life. Here guests stroll with cups in hand and savour the atmosphere of the cast-iron colonnade, the Singing Fountain and the surrounding pavilions. All three colonnade springs are only a few steps apart, on level ground with paved paths.

### Cross Spring (Křížový pramen)

The **Cross Spring** is Marienbad's most famous spring — the one where the history of the spa began. Locals had known the spring for ages, but it was the Teplá monastery physician Josef Jan Nehr who brought it fame when he pushed through the first spa buildings in the late 18th century. The cross that gave the spring its name has stood here since 1749. Today's neoclassical pavilion dates from 1912 and is the ceremonial heart of every drinking cure.

- **Where:** the Cross Spring pavilion at the upper end of the Main Colonnade
- **How to get there:** on foot from the centre of the spa quarter, on the level; the pavilion is covered and accessible even in rain
- **Character of the water:** acidulous water with Glauber's salt, markedly sparkling
- **Taste:** bitter, salty-mineral — the most pronounced of the colonnade springs
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Caroline Spring (Karolinin pramen)

The **Caroline Spring** was originally called the New Spring; it received the name of Empress Caroline Augusta in 1817. The neoclassical Caroline Spring colonnade, one of the town's most photographed structures, is dedicated to it.

- **Where:** in the ensemble of buildings around the Main Colonnade, a few steps from the Singing Fountain
- **How to get there:** a short level walk from the Cross Spring
- **Character of the water:** acidulous water with a high magnesium content
- **Taste:** milder than the Cross Spring, with a mineral finish
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Rudolph Spring (Rudolfův pramen)

The **Rudolph Spring** bears the name of Crown Prince Rudolph. It rises at its own pavilion in the lower part of town in Úšovice, but its water is also piped to the Cross Spring pavilion by the colonnade — so you can taste it conveniently in the heart of the spa quarter as well as at its source.

- **Where:** the Cross Spring pavilion (Rudolph Spring outlet) and the Rudolph Spring pavilion in Úšovice
- **How to get there:** effortlessly at the colonnade; the walk to the Úšovice pavilion leads through the lower town and can be combined with the Ferdinand and Anthony Springs
- **Character of the water:** acidulous water rich in calcium and magnesium
- **Taste:** mild, less salty than the Cross Spring
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

## Springs in the spa park and in the town

Along the promenades, in the parks and in the lower part of town you will find further springs, hidden in Empire-style pavilions and garden gazebos. The way to them is a pleasant walk through the park — and in Marienbad a walk with cup in hand is as important a part of the day as the spring itself.

### Ambrose Spring (Ambrožův pramen)

The **Ambrose Spring** has three outlets in the spa park below the colonnade, close to the Central Baths and below Goethe Square. Because of its iron content it was also called the "spring of love" in the 19th century — and Goethe himself is said to have liked resting beside it.

- **Where:** the spa park below the colonnade, by the Central Baths
- **How to get there:** a few minutes on foot from the colonnade along the park paths, gently downhill
- **Character of the water:** iron-rich acidulous water
- **Taste:** slightly metallic, fresh; the iron leaves a rusty deposit at the outlet
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Forest Spring (Lesní pramen)

The **Forest Spring** rises at the northern edge of the spa quarter by the Třebízský brook, and its Empire-style pavilion of 1869 is among the most graceful in the spa. For many guests, the morning walk to the Forest Spring with cup in hand is a daily ritual.

- **Where:** northern edge of the spa quarter, by the Třebízský brook
- **How to get there:** a walk of about a quarter of an hour from the colonnade through the park along the brook
- **Character of the water:** mild alkaline acidulous water — the mildest of the great drinking springs
- **Taste:** soft, low in salt, lightly sparkling
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Maria Spring (Mariin pramen)

The **Maria Spring** — the spring that gave the town its name — is not a spring in the classic sense. What rises from the depths here is not water but almost pure carbon dioxide (99.7%), "Maria's Gas", which only secondarily dissolves into the shallow groundwater. The sulphurous smell of the vent originally earned the place the name "Stinking Meadow", before it was renamed after an image of the Virgin Mary. It was in use even before the spa was founded.

- **Where:** behind the Central Baths
- **Character:** vent of natural CO₂ (99.7%)
- **Not for drinking:** the captured gas is used in the neighbouring Maria Spa spa house for dry CO₂ baths and gas injections; you cannot bathe in the vent
- **Worth knowing:** you can view the spring from outside as a monument, but "tasting" it means a spa house, not a cup

### Ferdinand Spring (Ferdinandův pramen)

The **Ferdinand Spring** is the oldest spring of Marienbad in written records — as early as the 16th century, in the reign of Ferdinand I, after whom it is named, salt-boiling was attempted here. The charming pavilion of 1827 stands in the lower part of town in Úšovice and makes a rewarding goal for an afternoon walk.

- **Where:** Úšovice, the lower part of town
- **How to get there:** a pleasant walk through Úšovice park from the spa quarter (allow about an hour there and back); you can taste the Anthony Spring on the way
- **Character of the water:** strong Glauber-salt acidulous water with the highest mineral content of all the Marienbad springs
- **Taste:** similar to the Cross Spring, but even more pronounced and saltier
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Anthony Spring (Antonínův pramen)

The **Anthony Spring** in Úšovice served the surrounding farmsteads long before the spa came into being; today's small pavilion dates from 1997, when the outlet was newly captured. Locals consider it the best "table" acidulous water and come here to fill their bottles.

- **Where:** Úšovice, on the way to the Ferdinand Spring
- **How to get there:** a stop on the walk through the lower town to the Ferdinand Spring
- **Character of the water:** refreshing, very drinkable acidulous water
- **Taste:** light, fresh — the least "mineral" of the springs listed here
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

## Forest springs — acidulous waters in the countryside

Those looking for springs away from the colonnade will find freely rising acidulous waters, without pavilions or opening hours, in the forests north of the town. The way to them doubles as a spa walk along marked trails; bring sturdy footwear and a cup.

### Farská Kyselka

**Farská Kyselka** is a freely rising acidulous spring in the forests north of the town and a classic goal of the spa walk. The iron-rich water leaves its typical rusty deposit at the outlet — a cup on the spot is part of the experience.

- **Where:** the forest north of the town, towards the village of Prameny
- **How to get there:** along the blue-marked trail from the colonnade, an easy walk of just under an hour; to extend the loop, continue to the Smraďoch reserve
- **Character of the water:** iron-rich forest acidulous water
- **Taste:** metallic, markedly sparkling
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

### Balbín Spring (Balbínův pramen)

The **Balbín Spring**, named after the scholar Bohuslav Balbín, rises in the Prameniště natural monument by the little Balbín pond, between the Hleďsebe quarter and the forests north of the town. The surrounding boggy meadows are among the last places where the rare sundew grows — so the spring deserves a quiet, considerate visit along the marked paths.

- **Where:** the Balbín peat bog, northern part of the town
- **How to get there:** on foot from the Hleďsebe quarter or as a detour from the forest paths; the ground is wet, keep to the footpaths
- **Character of the water:** gentle iron-rich acidulous water
- **Taste:** slightly metallic, delicate
- **Temperature:** cold, around 7–10 °C

## The spa cup and etiquette at the spring

{% figure
   src="/images/library/drinking-cure/elderly-man-drinking-cure.jpg"
   alt="A man drinks mineral water at a cast-iron fountain in the park"
   caption="A guest savours mineral water straight from a garden fountain."
   width="wide" /%}

- **The cup:** the traditional *spa cup* (lázeňský pohárek) is a porcelain mug with a flat handle that serves as a spout; you can buy one in the souvenir shops on the colonnade and nearby. Without a cup, your own beaker or bottle will do.
- **The physician's schedule:** anyone at the spa for a drinking cure drinks according to the spa physician's schedule — spring, quantity and time. Anyone merely tasting the springs can take a few sips at each and compare the taste.
- **Mornings at the colonnade:** the springs are busiest in the morning before breakfast, when the spa guests head for them. For peace and photographs without crowds, come later in the morning or in the early evening.
- **Slowly, and while walking:** the water is drunk in small sips during a slow walk, not all at once at the outlet — and do not linger at the outlet longer than necessary, so that others get their turn.
- **Bottles:** at the colonnade springs you drink from a cup; anyone wanting to take water away traditionally heads for the Anthony Spring in Úšovice, where locals go to fill their bottles.
- **Cold water:** the springs are cold all year round (around 7–10 °C); in winter bring gloves, as the porcelain chills quickly.

A detailed guide to how the drinking cure works is in the article [The drinking cure — a guide](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide), and the differences between the individual waters in the article [Guide to the mineral springs](/en/magazine/mineral-springs-complete-guide).

## Opening hours and access

Most springs are **freely accessible all year round** and the water is free. The pavilions of the colonnade springs are open, as a guide, from 6:00 to 18:00, in summer usually an hour longer; opening times change with the season, so check the current hours on the spot or at the [colonnade](/en/colonnade). The forest springs are accessible at any time; some smaller pavilions and gazebos outside the centre may be closed in winter.

A walk along all the main springs takes roughly **2–3 hours** and leads through the loveliest parts of the spa quarter, parks and forests — the ideal programme for a morning with cup in hand.
